; ODDP and EVENP are functions: interpreted, through FUNCALL and APPLY, and
; as #'ODDP (sys/sys2/numer.lisp, their DEFUNs).  sys/cold/defmic.lisp
; declares both misc instructions that are not Lisp functions, so the cold
; load gave them no function cell and every interpreted call signalled "The
; function ODDP is undefined."; compiled calls, the misc instruction, worked.
; Each case is evaluated by the interpreter, so run it WITHOUT --compile for
; its own forms; the change itself must be compiled into the band, since
; numer.lisp loaded as source would make each body call itself.  The cases
; marked "control" pass without the change.  The documentation cases want
; sys/cold/docmic.lisp loaded too, as source or compiled.
;
(fboundp 'oddp) => T
(fboundp 'evenp) => T
(eval '(oddp 3)) => T
(eval '(oddp 2)) => NIL
(eval '(evenp 2)) => T
(eval '(evenp 3)) => NIL
(eval '(oddp -3)) => T
(eval '(oddp -2)) => NIL
(eval '(evenp -2)) => T
(eval '(evenp -3)) => NIL
(eval '(oddp 0)) => NIL
(eval '(evenp 0)) => T
(eval '(oddp (1+ (expt 2 40)))) => T
(eval '(evenp (1+ (expt 2 40)))) => NIL
(eval '(evenp (- (expt 2 40)))) => T
(eval '(oddp (- 1 (expt 2 40)))) => T
(funcall #'evenp 4) => T
(funcall #'oddp 4) => NIL
(apply 'oddp '(7)) => T
(apply 'evenp '(7)) => NIL
(mapcar 'evenp '(1 2 3 4)) => (NIL T NIL T)
; the documentation, which sys/cold/docmic.lisp's string gives over the DEFUN's;
; ODDP's read "T is NUMBER is odd."  EVENP's is a control.
(documentation 'oddp 'function) => "T if NUMBER is odd."
(documentation 'evenp 'function) => "T if NUMBER is even."
; a number that is not an integer is refused, as by the misc instruction
(eval '(oddp 1.5)) =>! The argument to ODDP, 1.5, was of the wrong type
(eval '(evenp 1.5)) =>! The argument to EVENP, 1.5, was of the wrong type
; control: a compiled call, the misc instruction, worked before the change
(funcall (compile nil '(lambda (x) (list (oddp x) (evenp x)))) 5) => (T NIL)
